  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Monday)\nIssued at 1202 AM CDT Tue May 12 2026\n\nAn upper level ridge will move east across Texas Wednesday and \nWednesday night. Our area will be under quasi-zonal flow aloft \nThursday and Friday. A dryline will mix east to near our western \nborder counties Friday afternoon. Moisture availability looks too \nlimited at this time to support any chances for thunderstorms with \nthe dryline. Temperatures will be getting hotter in the mid-to-late \nweek time frame. For Friday, highs are expected to range from around \n90 in our southeastern counties, to 100-103 degrees in the northwest \nBig Country and portions of the northwest Concho Valley. A tighter \npressure gradient and lee surface troughing to our west will result \nin breezy south winds Thursday into the weekend.  \n\nThe weekend looks a little more unsettled. An upper shortwave trough \nis progged to move into Texas from the west-southwest on Saturday, \nwith a dryline mixing east across a good portion of West Texas. At \nthis time, carrying low (15-20 PoPs) Saturday evening and early \nSaturday night, for the northern third of our area, to account for \nthe possibility of a few thunderstorms developing and moving off the \ndryline into that area. With strengthening vertical shear and \nmoderate to possibly strong instability, the possibility of a few \nstrong to severe storms cannot be ruled out. With southwest flow \naloft over the area Sunday and the possibility of an embedded wave \nentering the area, carrying low (20-30 PoPs) for showers and storms \nSunday afternoon into Sunday night in portions of our area. \nTemperatures will be above normal over the weekend, and development \nof a nocturnal low-level jet will result in nights warm and more \nhumid.   \n\nThunderstorm chances look to increase Monday afternoon and Monday\nnight when a cold front is progged to move south into the area \nwith an incoming shortwave aloft. Details in timing of the front \nand placement/strength of the shortwave trough are uncertain with \nthis being Day 7 of the forecast.",
